
Let's go through the entire file, line by line, from top to bottom.

yaml
version: "3.9"

This tells Docker which version of the Compose file format you're using. It's just a technical label so Docker knows how to read the rest of the file correctly. You don't need to think about this much — just keep it there.

yaml
services:

This is the starting point that says "here comes the list of containers I want to run." Everything below this, indented, is a separate container.

First container: app
yaml
  app:

This names your first container app — this is going to be your actual Node.js application.

yaml
    build: .

This says "don't pull a ready-made image, build one from my own Dockerfile, which is sitting in the current folder (.)." This is how your custom code becomes a container.

yaml
    ports:
      - "3000:3000"

This connects port 3000 inside the container to port 3000 on your real computer. Without this, your app would be running, but you couldn't visit it at localhost:3000 in your browser — it'd be sealed inside with no door out.

yaml
    depends_on:
      - db
      - redis

This tells Docker: "before starting app, make sure db and redis have already started." Makes sense, because your app tries to connect to MongoDB and Redis the moment it boots up — if they're not ready yet, it would fail.

yaml
    env_file:
      - .env

This loads all your secret values (MONGO_URI, PORT, etc.) from your .env file into this container, so your code can read them using process.env.MONGO_URI.

Second container: db
yaml
  db:

Names this container db — this is your MongoDB database.

yaml
    image: mongo:7

Instead of building this from your own Dockerfile, just download the official MongoDB image (version 7) that MongoDB's own team maintains on Docker Hub. You're not writing MongoDB's code — you're just using their pre-built container.

yaml
    restart: always

If this container crashes, or your computer restarts, automatically start it again. Keeps your database always available.

yaml
    environment:
      - MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_USERNAME=user
      - MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_PASSWORD=pass
      - MONGO_INITDB_DATABASE=mydb

This sets up MongoDB's login the very first time the container is created — like registering a username, password, and creating a database named mydb. These three variable names (MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_USERNAME etc.) are fixed by MongoDB's official image — but the values (user, pass, mydb) are yours to choose.

yaml
    volumes:
      - db_data:/data/db

MongoDB stores its actual data at /data/db inside the container. But containers are temporary — if deleted, everything inside is lost. This line says: "save that folder outside the container, in a named storage space called db_data, so the data survives even if the container gets rebuilt."

Third container: redis
yaml
  redis:

Names this container redis.

yaml
    image: redis:7

Same idea as MongoDB — pull the official Redis image (version 7) instead of building it yourself.

yaml
    restart: always

Same as before — auto-restart if it crashes.

Bottom of the file
yaml
volumes:
  db_data:

This officially registers db_data as a real storage volume Docker manages. It matches the name you used earlier in volumes: - db_data:/data/db — this line is just declaring "yes, db_data is a real thing, create and persist it."

Putting it all together — what happens when you run it
bash
docker compose up --build
Docker builds the app container from your Dockerfile
Docker downloads and starts the official mongo:7 container, sets up the login you specified
Docker downloads and starts the official redis:7 container
All three containers can now talk to each other using their service names (db, redis) as hostnames — that's why your .env uses db:27017 and not localhost:27017
Your app becomes visible at http://localhost:3000 in your browser
